<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => (element == null) => this.get(index) == null || this.get(index).equals(element);</code>

However, the above assert statement doesn't quite capture the intended logic correctly due to the structure. The correct interpretation should be that if the element is null, the element at the index should be null after insertion, otherwise, it should equal the inserted element. Here's the corrected version:

<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => (element == null) ? this.get(index) == null : this.get(index).equals(element);</code>

But since the ternary operator might not be directly supported in JML, we can rephrase it using logical implications:

<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => ((element == null) => this.get(index) == null) && ((element != null) => this.get(index).equals(element));</code>

This ensures that if the element is null, the element at the index is null, and if the element is not null, the element at the index equals the inserted element.
